Final Model
The workflow-set comparison above told us the random forest wins. Let’s fit it once more on the full training set so we have a single model object to interrogate:

final &lt;- workflow() |&gt;
  add_recipe(rec) |&gt;
  add_model(rf_model) |&gt;
  fit(data = camels_train)

Define 3 models (30) — Week 6-1

Define a random forest model using the rand_forest function (engine ranger, mode regression)
Define two other models of your choice. The Week 6-1 zoo (linear_reg, boost_tree, svm_rbf, svm_poly, mlp, bag_mlp, decision_tree) is a good menu — the comparison table in that deck summarizes strengths and weaknesses.
For each model, briefly note (one sentence) why it’s a reasonable candidate for this regression problem — what kind of structure does it capture?

Random Forest Model

Random_Forest &lt;- rand_forest() |&gt;
  set_engine("ranger", importance = "impurity") |&gt;
  set_mode("regression")

2 Other Models:

XGB_Model &lt;- boost_tree() |&gt;
  set_engine("xgboost") |&gt;
  set_mode("regression")

LM_Model &lt;- linear_reg() |&gt;
  set_engine("lm") |&gt;
  set_mode("regression")


The random forest model was selected because it can capture complex nonlinear interactions within hydrologic data without definition. The XGBoost model was chosen because this method improves predictive accuracy through error correction, while also handling nonlinear relationships. Lastly, the linear model was included as a simpler approach to compare how well linear relationships explain streamflow relative to the other methods.
